==English==

{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|scrūpulus||uneasiness of mind, trouble, anxiety, doubt, scruple, literally a small sharp or pointed stone, the twenty-fourth part of an ounce|lang=la}}, diminutive of {{term|scrūpus||a rough or sharp stone, anxiety, uneasiness|lang=la}}; perhaps akin to {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|σκύρος|sc=polytonic|tr=skuros|lang=grc||the chippings of stone}}, {{term|ξυρόν||a razor|tr=ksuron|lang=grc}}, {{etyl|sa|en}} {{term|क्षुर|tr=kṣura||a razor|lang=sa}}: compare {{etyl|fr|en}} ''[[scrupule]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈskɹuː.pəl/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-scruple.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːpəl|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A weight of twenty [[grain]]s; the third part of a [[dram]].
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} Hence, a very small quantity; a particle.
#* Ca '''1601–1608''', [[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]], ''As You Like It'', Act II Scene 3 221–222
#*: Paroles: ''I have not, my lord, deserved it.'' Lafeu: ''Yes, good faith, ev'ry dram of it, and I will not bate thee a '''scruple'''.''
# Hesitation as to action from the difficulty of determining what is right or expedient; unwillingness, doubt, or hesitation proceeding from motives of conscience.
#: ''He was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his '''scruples'''.'' - [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A doubt or uncertainty concerning a matter of fact; intellectual perplexity.
# A measurement of time. Hebrew culture broke the hour into 1080 scruples.

